TI  - Blockchain-Enabled Intellectual Property Management: A Comprehensive Framework for Digital Rights Protection
PY  - 2025
T2  - 2025 IEEE International Conference on Blockchain and Distributed Systems Security, ICBDS 2025
DO  - 10.1109/ICBDS67396.2025.11377097
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105034438002?origin=resultslist
AD  - Pimpri-Chinchwad College of Engineering, Dept. of Computer Engineering, Pune, India
AB  - The digital transformation of intellectual property (IP) ecosystems has created pressing challenges in establishing provenance, preventing infringement, and managing rights in distributed environments. Blockchain technology emerges as a disruptive solution with its decentralized architecture, cryptographic security, and immutable audit trails. This paper presents a comprehensive examination of blockchain applications in intellectual property management, analyzing 17 distinct use cases across eight industry verticals. We develop a novel taxonomy of blockchain-based IP systems, comparing six architectural paradigms and their suitability for different IP classes. The study evaluates twelve commercial and governmental implementations, identifying key success factors and limitations. Through detailed analysis of three consensus mechanisms and four smart contract frameworks, we establish technical guidelines for system designers. The paper further examines seven critical challenges including legal recognition, scalability constraints, and interoperability barriers, proposing a roadmap for future research. Our findings demonstrate that hybrid blockchain architectures with off-chain storage and on-chain verification currently offer the most viable solution for enterprise-scale IP management. The research contributes a validated framework for assessing blockchain implementations in IP ecosystems, supported by case studies from patent offices, creative industries, and manufacturing sectors.  Β© 2025 IEEE.
