AB  - Bulgariaβ€™s electoral system has shown persistent machine-voting vulnerabilities, including unverifiable tallies, protocol errors, and registration inconsistencies reported by the OSCE and the Constitutional Court. These issues reveal the necessity of transparent, auditable, and secure voting mechanisms. The purpose of this paper is to evaluate whether blockchain-based systems can provide a technically feasible framework for national electronic voting in Bulgaria. Three blockchain voting systems-Helios, Voatz, and FollowMyVote-are analysed to identify solutions for Bulgariaβ€™s electoral system. Based on this analysis, the paper proposes a blockchain e-voting framework that combines cryptographic verification, institutional validators, and smart contract automation to meet national requirements for transparency, accuracy, and trust. These requirements remain within the capabilities of current permissioned blockchain platforms. The contribution is a context-aware evaluation framework that establishes technical feasibility and outlines directions for pilot implementation and future research. Future work could involve testing individual modules on a small scale, such as authentication, tallying and auditability. This would involve examining risks, including security vulnerabilities, usability challenges and institutional barriers. Β© 2025 IEEE.
