AB  - The permanent, secure, and trustworthy preservation of digital heritage archives presents a significant challenge for cultural inheritance. Addressing core issues in traditional centralized management systems, such as data vulnerability to tampering, lack of provenance information, and fragile trust mechanisms, this paper proposes a Blockchain-based Distributed Digital Heritage Archive Provenance and Integrity Model (BDHAPI). The model adopts a 'Blockchain + IPFS' collaborative architecture. It utilizes IPFS (InterPlanetary File System) for the distributed storage of archive data entities, resolving the blockchain's storage bottleneck. Cryptographic hash functions are used to lock the archive content's 'digital fingerprint'. The core contribution lies in the design of a smart contract to construct a structured, immutable full life-cycle Provenance Event Chain (PE) on the blockchain, enabling the automated and trustless execution of archive registration, integrity verification, and provenance queries. To validate the model's effectiveness, a comparative experiment was conducted on the Hyperledger Fabric consortium blockchain platform using the Europeana public digital heritage dataset. Experimental results show that, compared to a traditional Centralized Database (CDB) and a simplified blockchain solution (BC-Simple), the BDHAPI model has reasonable overhead in write and query latency but exhibits an absolute advantage in security. It achieves 100% detection of tampering with data, metadata, and provenance logs, providing a highly trustworthy data integrity assurance and a transparent, traceable chain of evidence for digital heritage archives. Β© 2025 IEEE.
