AB  - Forgery, unnecessary change and low cross institutional interoperability are the key security threats that need to be considered by digital prescription systems. To address these issues and meet the privacy and regulation requirements that pertain to a patient, the new blockchain prescription validation framework is suggested in this paper. The proposed system developed with the use of security cryptographic hashes storage within the Ethereum blockchain with a smart contract and with the tamper-proof verification of the prescription without revealing the Protected Health Information (PHI). The structure employs a full workflow of prescription issues verified with the help of Metamask, the generation of the SHA-256 hash, and the onchain storage of the store data with the assistance of Solidity smart contract solutions and its subsequent authenticity. The cryptocurrencies served by the proof of authorities (PoA) system can be explained by the fact that the Ethereum network evidenced the confirming time under a second and low-cost gas-using operations with fixed-size hashes. Role prosecution - Safety study presents a formidable resistance to typical violations involving prescription forgery, replay assaults, and not authorization modifications alongside role-based access management. The complete audit trails are kept, and patient confidentiality is not rendered vulnerable since cryptographic digest is on-chain only. When compared to longer striking interoperability in the health institution sector, transparency, destruction of a single point of failure, and even a more improved interoperability is better. This research is a feasible, and scalable fraud prevention software within the digital healthcare system, forms a groundwork to further usage of blockchain in the sphere of medical records handling.  Β© 2025 IEEE.
