AB  - Organ transplantation faces major challenges, including organ shortages, inefficient allocation, and ethical concerns. This study proposes a blockchain-based framework to enhance transparency, security, and efficiency in transplantation workflows. By leveraging decentralised ledgers, smart contracts, and cryptographic techniques, the system addresses data tampering, traceability gaps, and allocation delays. The multi-layered architecture integrates immutable records, automated matching, role-based interfaces, and IoT-enabled organ tracking. Simulation results demonstrate a 75% reduction in matching time compared to a conventional manual allocation system, as well as a 92.5% decrease in errors. The framework also supports ethical compliance and auditability and reduces illicit organ trade through verifiable custody chains. While promising, the approach faces challenges related to scalability, Electronic Health Records (EHR) interoperability, and regulatory harmonisation. Future directions include pilot implementations and Layer-2 enhancements for broader deployment. Β© 2017 IEEE.
