AB  - In the rush of EHR digitalization, patient data security becomes critical, highlighting small clinics that must contend with the risks of a more integrated healthcare system. A single point of failure is brought about by the current centralized system and conventional password- based authentication methods. In addition, patient consent is limited to a single consent for EHR sharing. In order to overcome these constraints, this study suggests a blockchain-enabled framework that incorporates zero- knowledge proofs (ZKPs) for access control over healthcare data that protects privacy. While blockchain smart contracts oversee immutable audit trails and consent processes, huge medical files are kept on IPFS. To demonstrate characteristics like patient permission or eligibility without disclosing underlying personal information ZKP circuits are created using Circom and Snarkjs. A Solidity, Hardhat and Web3 prototype shows how medical professionals can safely access records created by the Internet of Things (IoT) only once legitimate evidence is provided. Transparency, integrity and confidentiality are enhanced with the realistic gas and latency performance, according to experimental evaluation. This work combines blockchain immutability with contemporary cryptographic verification approaches to help and create reliable as well as interoperable healthcare ecosystems. Β© 2025 IEEE.
