AB  - This chapter explores the transformative potential of blockchain technology for electronic health records (EHRs), addressing critical limitations of current systems such as data silos, security vulnerabilities, lack of patient control, and interoperability challenges. It examines how core blockchain featuresβ€”decentralization, immutability, cryptographic security, and smart contractsβ€”can enable more secure, patient-centric, and interoperable health data management. The discussion covers architectural models, key functionalities like granular access control and immutable audit trails, privacy-enhancing techniques, and the significant benefits of enhanced security, data integrity, and patient empowerment. While acknowledging implementation hurdles like scalability, regulatory compliance, and integration complexity, the chapter analyzes real-world applications and future trends, concluding that blockchain offers a foundational pathway toward realizing the vision of truly smart, connected, and patient-owned healthcare records within the broader smart healthcare ecosystem. Β© 2026 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
