AB  - Healthcare insurance fraud is a substantial danger to the financial viability of healthcare systems, resulting in yearly losses up to billions. Conventional fraud detection systems face challenges related to data manipulation, delayed recognition, and insufficient openness. The proposed study presents a hybrid AI-driven architecture that integrates Blockchain technology, Deep Neural Networks (DNNs), and AdaBoost ensemble learning to improve detection accuracy, data security, and real-time responsiveness. Blockchain guarantees data immutability and traceability through smart contracts and cryptographic hash functions, whereas the DNN identifies complex fraud patterns in insurance claims. AdaBoost enhances categorisation by boosting weak learners trained on DNN outputs. The suggested system was evaluated using curated healthcare fraud datasets, attaining 98.4% accuracy, 97.8% precision, 98.0% recall, and 99.3% AUC-ROC, outperforming conventional models such as Logistic Regression, SVM, and Random Forest. This hybrid method offers a scalable and secure system for effectively identifying fraudulent claims while maintaining trust among all stakeholders. The findings indicate substantial potential for implementation in extensive insurance systems, providing considerable improvements in fraud prevention, operational efficiency, and audit security. Future enhancements may involve federated architectures and real-time mobile integration for cross-institutional fraud detection systems. Β© 2025 IEEE.
