AB  - The transition towards sustainable smart cities necessitates the deployment of advanced energy management frameworks capable of optimizing distributed energy resources (DERs) through decentralized trading mechanisms. This paper proposes a Blockchain-Enabled Peer-to-Peer (P2P) Energy Trading Framework aimed at enhancing energy efficiency, resilience, and sustainability within urban centers of Malaysia. Leveraging a consortium blockchain architecture, the proposed framework integrates smart contracts, decentralized ledger technology (DLT), and cryptographic techniques to facilitate transparent, tamper-proof, and real-time energy transactions between prosumers and consumers. The system architecture is designed to address critical challenges associated with energy trading in Malaysia, including scalability, privacy preservation, latency reduction, and regulatory compliance. Smart contracts are utilized to autonomously enforce trading agreements, ensuring transactional integrity and minimizing the need for intermediaries. Furthermore, a dual-layer consensus mechanism combining Proof-of-Authority (PoA) and Practical Byzantine Fault Tolerance (PBFT) is proposed to enhance system throughput and security while maintaining low computational overhead. To validate the efficacy of the framework, simulations are conducted using real-world energy consumption and production data from Malaysian urban environments. Performance metrics such as transaction latency, throughput, scalability, energy cost reduction, and security resilience are rigorously evaluated. Results indicate that the proposed framework achieves significant improvements in energy efficiency, transactional transparency, and cost-effectiveness when compared to conventional centralized energy trading systems. Β© The Institution of Engineering & Technology 2025.
