AD  - Baoji Vocational Institute of Technology, Fengxiang Normal College, Shaanxi, Baoji, 721013, China
AB  - In innovative park backgrounds, the way of preserving, managing, and enhancing intangible cultural heritage (ICH) is revolutionized by the integration of digital twin technology (DTT) and new 6G networks. For identifying, simulating, and transmitting real-time cultural experience, an adaptive, realistic, and safe interaction systems are needed for ICH in contrast to physical resources. To handle secure interactions in ICH parks over 6G networks, a standardized digital twin framework was suggested in this study. This standardized digital twin framework integrates a multi-layer architecture with Sensing layer, virtualization layer, communication layer, and application layer. The ultra-low latency, terahertz connectivity of 6G technologies, and AI-driven resource allocation are utilized by the model. By this implementation, seamless interaction among physical cultural activities and digital counterparts, then real-time synchronization of performances, rituals, and storytelling are all facilitated by the model. This model also preserves accessibility and reliability. The integration of lightweight cryptographic protocols, blockchain-based trust management, and context-aware access control are integrated into the system for the purpose of resolving security issues. Thus, sensitive cultural data is secured. The integration components particularly adhere to IEEE 6G and ITU-T standards, UNESCO and ISO digital heritage rules, so it is essential. Various sensors, devices, and platforms are interacted with one another, and global engagement with cultural heritage is facilitated. When comparing this suggested model with conventional digital heritage systems, the suggested model attains maximum transmission accuracy, low end-to-end delay, and strong resilience to protect against cyberattacks, and it was demonstrated by the outcomes. In digital age, ICH is maintained by the suggested model. The cultural tourism, remote participation, and long-term preservation is facilitated by this suggested model. For sustainable, secure, and smart management of cultural resources in 6G-enabled backgrounds, a standards-compliant reference model was offered by the suggested model to policymakers, cultural organizations, and network designers. Β© 2017 IEEE.
