AD  - Department of Computer Application, CKD Institute of Management & Technology, Punjab, Amritsar, India
AB  - Achieving seamless interoperability and effective health information exchange (HIE) remains a critical yet elusive goal in modern healthcare, hampered by fragmented systems, data silos, privacy concerns, and inconsistent standards. This chapter explores the transformative potential of blockchain technology in addressing these persistent challenges. It examines how blockchain's core principles of decentralization, cryptographic security, immutability, and consensus can create a secure, transparent, and auditable foundation for HIE. The chapter details how blockchain facilitates granular patient consent management, enhances data provenance and integrity, improves patient identity matching, and enables secure data sharing across disparate stakeholders without requiring a central trusted authority. It analyzes potential architectural models, integration strategies with existing standards like HL7 FHIR, and the significant benefits of increased data liquidity, patient empowerment, and reduced administrative friction. However, the chapter also critically addresses key implementation hurdles, including scalability, regulatory compliance (e.g., HIPAA, GDPR), governance complexities, and technical maturity. By reviewing emerging use cases and future directions, the chapter argues that blockchain offers a promising, albeit evolving, pathway toward realizing the vision of truly patient-centric, interoperable healthcare ecosystems. Β© 2026 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
