AB  - In the Internet-of-Medical-Things (IoMT) context, confidentiality and integrity of patientsβ€™ sensitive medical records in transit over insecure transmission channels are primary concerns. The rapid growth in Internet-of-Things (IoT) applications in the medical field has paved the way for the evolution of the IoMT. Out of medical data, medical images constitute a core component. Therefore, medical image encryption is a critical aspect of ensuring the privacy of medical data. While many encryption schemes are capable of resisting multiple types of attacks, many fail to meet the real-time constraints of medical systems. Medical image confidentiality has been tackled utilizing cost-efficient DNA-based cryptography, elliptic curve encryption, blockchain-based methodology, and access control-based cryptography; however, these techniques often experience adaptability and complexity challenges. Chaos-based cryptographic systems provide a high level N of security via nonlinear dynamics, but are constrained by hardware practical realization and vulnerability to certain attack scenarios. This work presents an Enigma Chaotic Map (ECM) based, robust, and computationally efficient cryptosystem for medical image encryption. The proposed novel ECM exhibits improved statistical properties with low computational complexity. ECM employs cascading, switching mechanisms, and conditional progressive scaling modulation, yielding a larger chaotic range and better security performance. The proposed cryptosystem is thoroughly evaluated utilizing formal security estimations, informal robustness evaluation, and detailed performance estimation. The empirical evidence showed an average correlation coefficient (0.0013), Shannon entropy (7.9974), NPCR (99.61 %), and UACI (33.49 %), affirming an advanced level of security. Moreover, the proposed cryptosystem attains an asymptotic complexity of (Formula presented) execution time 0.007 s for (Formula presented) images, memory utilization of 65.86 KB, and a throughput (8.93 MB/s), confirming low computational, and energy overhead, making it appropriate for resource constraint IoMT applications. Β© 2026 Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
