AB  - As journalism moves deeper online, the dissemination of misinformation and disinformation is corroding trust and impacting the ability to conduct democratic processes. Current tools for verification are failing to impede the spread of false information. This paper explores the extent to which the blockchain might contribute to the protocols and processes embedded in this β€trustless environment and offers the potential for the development of immutable, timestamped, provenance and delivery of published news that might succeed in tilting truth to the extent that circulating falsehood will come to be seen as futile, given the extent to which fake news is spreading so rapidly across digital environments. In particular, the paper considers the extent to which timestamping, cryptographic proof and decentralized storage might be harnessed to enhance the quality of information that surfaces and circulates in and through the net, as well as how transactional features such as smart contracts might be exploited to automate the verification of the authenticity and trustworthiness of published content. The concluding remarks in this paper argue that the potential for digital transformation is enormous, but also that this might be digital technology that will enter the arena with enormous difficulty, given the lengthy list of technical, ethical and scalability issues that require attention. Finally, the paper argues that blockchain holds great promise with the aim of reinstating the credibility of digital information. Β© 2026 selection and editorial matter, Deepti Dabas Hazarika, Arnab Chakraborty, Shiwangi Sharma, Apurva A. Chauhan and Neha Yadav; individual chapters, the contributors
