AB  - Healthcare data sharing across institutions presents major challenges related to privacy, security, and trust, as sensitive patient information must comply with regulations such as HIPAA and GDPR. Conventional centralized learning techniques reveal patient information to possible breaches and unauthorized access, which is inapplicable to healthcare analytics on a large scale. In order to overcome these drawbacks, the current study suggests a Blockchain-Integrated Federated Learning (FL) system that allows training a model in a decentralized manner with guaranteeing confidentiality to data and verifiable integrity. The system uses federated learning model that uses Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) and secure aggregation and differential privacy to safeguard the local model parameters in the process of training. The option of blockchain technology is included to reflect model changes based on cryptographic hashing and smart contracts to offer a transparent and immutable registry of collaboration between healthcare nodes. The effectiveness of the proposed solution is proven by experimental results, which showed an impressive 97 percent classification accuracy, low blockchain-imposed latency and high privacy preservation metrics. Also, scalability assessment shows that the accuracy of the model grows with the size of the client base involved, which proves the flexibility and strength of the framework. On the whole, this federated framework built on blockchain-based data privacy, trust, and performance as well as guarantees auditability and transparency, thus making it a secure and trustworthy approach to next-generation privacy-sensitive healthcare AI systems. Β© 2026 IEEE.
