AB  - Despite greater attention on the safety of electronic well-being archives and efforts by big towns worldwide to develop smart city setup, patients' private data is often compromised by information breaches. Previous attempts to address the issue have resulted in most patient data being unavailable. Current record supervision systems fail to balance data confidentiality and the necessity for affected persons and clinicians to relate with information frequently. Blockchain technology is a relatively innovation that allows reorganized and transactional data exchange. Blockchain technology may be used to strike a subtle balance between confidentiality and convenience for automated health information in the healthcare sector. This study aims to provide a blockchain-based architecture that protects the confidentiality of affected individuals' sensitive data while ensuring interoperable and efficient admission to medicinal data by donors, impacted individuals, and third parties. Our platform leverages intelligent agreements embedded in a Hyperledger-based blockchain to provide enhanced admittance control and data complication, as well as sophisticated cryptographic approaches for further protection. The purpose of this article is to examine how health care data cooperate with the diverse demands of affected persons, benefactors, and third parties, as well as to get an understanding of how the framework may solve long-standing confidentiality and security problems in the health care business. Β© 2025 IEEE.
