AB  - Modern cloud infrastructures emphasize scalability and cost-effectiveness, yet traditional security protocols often suffer from opaque operations, limited scalability, and susceptibility to tampering. This paper proposes a novel blockchain-based security framework that integrates immutable audit trails, smart contracts, and decentralized storage to enhance transparency and resilience in cloud environments. The architecture embeds cryptographic validation and consensus mechanisms into access control and logging processes, promoting tamper-resistant accountability. The study begins with a comprehensive analysis of conventional cloud security models, highlighting systemic issues such as centralized control and fragmented oversight. In response, a decentralized architecture is introduced, utilizing blockchain's audit capabilities and peer-to-peer storage networks to enforce policy autonomously and eliminate single points of failure. A prototype implementation is deployed across multi-cloud platforms and evaluated under high-load conditions. Benchmark results demonstrate a 40% improvement in access control update speed and a 92% reduction in dispute resolution time compared to centralized systems. By decentralizing trust through cryptographic proofs and consensus algorithms, the proposed model mitigates insider threats and data spoofing risks without compromising performance. These findings suggest that blockchain-enhanced frameworks hold significant promise for secure, scalable, and self-regulating cloud infrastructures. Β© 2025 IEEE.
