AD  - Saveetha Institute of Medical and Technical Sciences, Saveetha School of Engineering, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Tamilnadu, Thandalam, Chennai, India
AD  - Saveetha Institute of Medical and Technical Sciences, Saveetha School of Engineering, Department of Electronics and Communication and Engineering, Tamilnadu, Thandalam, Chennai, India
AB  - The Internet of Things (IoT) implementation experience has escalated the security, privacy and scalability concerns and there is more worry that the advent and execution of quantum computers will put the classical cryptography algorithms RSA and ECC at risk. The study deals with the acute problem of providing security solutions to IoT data transmission in a post-quantum world, where latticebased post-quantum cryptography (Kyber-512 cryptography encryption, Dilithium signatures) is combined with a streamlined blockchain system. The given system would encrypt IoT sensor data through quantum-proof algorithms and record hashbooks on the blockchain and secure data confidentiality, integrity, and authenticity. The low-latency, resource-friendly, energy-efficient verification capability of the proposed consensus protocol is optimized based on Practical Byzantine Fault Tolerance (PBFT) consensus protocol. Evaluation Experimental evidence has shown encryption accuracy of at least 99.97%, low bit error rates, and deterministic low computation time (2.4-3.90 ms encryption, 2.6-4.10 ms decryption at varying packet sizes. Blockchain throughput is set up at 230 Tx/ s where the finality times are below 1.5 s at a 5 KB block size. The findings validate the possibility of scalable, quantum-resistant and tamper proof IoT communication that is essentially future-proofing against new quantum threats to critical infrastructures. Β© 2025 IEEE.
