AB  - Carbon emission accounting (CEA) faces inherent challenges in balancing transparency, privacy, and efficiency, particularly in multi-stakeholder ecosystems where sensitive operational data must remain confidential while ensuring tamper-proof auditability. This paper proposes a blockchain-based CEA framework that integrates privacy-preserving computation and a novel no-proof consensus mechanism (NPC) to address these challenges. Leveraging the Paillier homomorphic encryption scheme, the framework encrypts emission parameters at the source, enabling secure aggregation and verification of results without exposing raw data. The NPC mechanism eliminates resource-intensive proofgeneration steps, allowing decentralized validation of encrypted accounting outcomes through pre-embedded cryptographic evidence. The case study demonstrates the system's ability and its potential to enable scalable, privacy-aware CEA. Β© 2025 IEEE.
