AB  - The global organ transplantation system grapples with persistent challenges surrounding equitable allocation, verifiable transparency, and optimal donor-recipient compatibility. To address these critical issues, an innovative framework combining decentralized technologies with advanced machine learning techniques has been developed. This solution establishes a secure ecosystem where medical institutions can participate in organ matching while maintaining complete data privacy. At its core, graph neural networks process complex immunological markers and biological compatibility factors, transforming them into multidimensional representations that capture intricate donorrecipient relationships more effectively than conventional matching algorithms. These advanced pattern recognition capabilities integrate with a distributed ledger infrastructure that records every transaction immutably, creating an auditable trail from initial matching to final transplantation. Cryptographic techniques ensure sensitive patient information remains protected throughout the process, with verification mechanisms that validate matches without exposing underlying personal data. Our innovative framework combines decentralized technologies with explainable AI, demonstrating superior performance through comparative analysis with XGBoost (0.94 vs 0.88 AUC-ROC) and Random Forest (0.94 vs 0.85 AUC-ROC). The system leverages PGExplainer-GNNs to process immunological markers, with HLAB*07:02 emerging as the most predictive factor (28 % contribution, Ξ©=0. 6 8), while blockchain infrastructure ensures verifiable transparency through ZK-SNARK proofs (21 kGwei gas cost). Federated learning enables privacy-preserving knowledge sharing (10-12 MB / round), improving matching accuracy by 14 % over conventional systems. Real-world deployment shows 99.97% uptime and 4 5 0 m s prediction speeds, with clinical validation confirming 30% lower rejection rates for GNN-matched transplants versus XGBoost-matched cases. This multidisciplinary approach represents a significant advancement in transplantation technology, harmonizing cutting-edge computer science with clinical best practices to save more lives through better organ matching. Β© 2026 IEEE.
