AB  - Large amounts of sensitive medical data have been produced by the quick digitization of healthcare and the growth of e-health services, raising worries about security, privacy, and regulatory compliance. The need for more robust cybersecurity solutions is highlighted by the risks that traditional cloud-based storage models expose Electronic Health Records (EHRs) to, including tampering, data leakage, and unauthorized access. The Blockchain-Powered Cyber Defense Framework for Privacy Preservation in Healthcare Informatics is presented in this paper. It combines patientcentric access control, decentralized storage, and cutting-edge cryptography to guarantee safe data management. The framework uses Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) for finegrained access management, Proof-of-Work (PoW) consensus for immutable and auditable record-keeping, and AES-Diffie-Hellman key exchange for secure communication. Furthermore, patient data is protected by SHA-256 access policies enabled by smart contracts, encrypted using a hybrid cryptographic algorithm, and stored in the InterPlanetary File System (IPFS). In comparison to RSA, NTRU, and DES, experimental results show a 97.9% decrease in instances of unauthorized access and enhanced encryption/decryption efficiency. Additionally, the optimized search mechanism facilitates safe and easy data sharing among authorized stakeholders while lowering computational complexity. The suggested framework creates a scalable, sustainable, and future-ready cybersecurity architecture for healthcare informatics by fusing the immutability of blockchain technology with privacy-preserving cryptography and patient-driven governance, guaranteeing the availability, confidentiality, and integrity of medical records.  Β© 2025 IEEE.
