AB  - Blockchain technology in a healthcare information sharing system would enhance security, privacy, and scalability to furnish better results. In this paper, the proposed framework is designed for privacy-preserving secure sharing of electronic health records based on blockchain technology. The architecture explicitly explicates IPFS-based decentralized storage and smart contracts along with permissioned blockchain for access control and patient consent, implemented, of course, with AES-256 and SHA-256 cryptographic algorithms. While ensuring confidentiality and integrity of information, conditions like those set under the HIPAA and GDPR acts enable AES-256 and SHA-256 mechanisms for data protection from unauthorized access. This would only be observed in the synthetic data used to compile the results considered as measure of its throughput, latency, and scalability potential. In the end, the framework is capable of translating the transactions through to 200 TPS per single node, after which the latency effect becomes growingly linear with the number of nodes. The framework also scales well. As more EHRs are added and once optimization is carried out for largescale deployments, more specifically, throughput and latency, to meet demands-this would make a fine, workable installation possibly with minor drawbacks and some losses due to the synthetic data in its description. Such differences will not compromise the actual performance of the framework. In the real aspects of the application, it shows great potential in solving major challenges in healthcare data sharing, such as security, privacy, and regulatory compliance.  Β© 2025 IEEE.
