AB  - Public budgeting systems suffer from transparency and accountability deficiencies, characterized by fragmented interoperability, incomplete audit trails, and manual workflows enabling data inconsistencies and approval delays. This research proposes a permissioned blockchain architecture employing Proof-of-Authority (PoA) consensus with institutionally verified validators to establish tamperresistant smart budgeting infrastructure. The framework designates Internal Audit Unit (IAU) and Public Financial Management (PFM) as authorized validators, restricting transaction approval to legitimate government entities through cryptographic authentication. A hybrid off-chain to on-chain mechanism preserves existing administrative processes while anchoring verified data onto immutable ledger via smart contract automation. Experimental validation deployed Solidity-based smart contracts on Ganache testnet configured to replicate Polygon network behavior, executing 100 sequential transaction cycles encompassing budget submission, dual-validator consensus, and realization tracking. Performance evaluation demonstrates consistent processing latency averaging 1020 milliseconds per workflow stage, validating deterministic transaction finality without computational overhead accumulation. The architecture establishes foundational infrastructure for transparent, accountable digital governance, validated through controlled testnet implementation in multi-agency public financial management environments. Β© 2026 IEEE.
