AB  - The study explores blockchain-based trust systems as a powerful tool of assuring online deals in contemporary decentralized systems. Through secondary data and application of a quantitative method of analysis, the study assesses the main determinants of trust, including the reliability of consensus, integrity of transaction, the cryptographic strength, and the time required to validate the transaction. To measure trust in a systematic way in a composite Trust Score model, a three-layer analytical model and the Blockchain Trust Evaluation Algorithm (BTEA) were created to calculate a trust score to compare blockchain systems systematically. Those results indicate that integrity, robustness of cryptographic techniques, and efficient consensus schemes as well as lower latency are highly beneficial to blockchain network trust. Besides, the suspense of advanced consensus models like BFT and PoS is identified in the analysis as superior in providing secure, transparent, and tamper-resistance environments in transactions. In general, the study confirms that blockchain is a disruptive technology that can enhance the security, reliability of the system, and scalable digital ecosystems, which can be seen as further evidence of its increased applicability in the digital infrastructure in the future.  Β© 2026 IEEE.
