AB  - Public procurement in Ecuador continues to face vulnerabilities to corruption and inefficiencyd espite the integration of advanced anti-fraud mechanisms, particularly during document submission and contractor selection. This paper proposes a novel system that integrates blockchain technology with the InterPlanetary File System (IPFS) to address these challenges. The hybrid architecture combines a private blockchain-restricted to verified institutional nodes f or managing submissions and contract execution through smart con-tracts-with a public blockchain that allows real-time auditability by external stakeholders. Blockchain ensures cryptographic security and immutable audit trails, while IPFS provides decentralized, scalable, and cost-effective storage beyond blockchain's intrinsic limitations. The proposed framework is designed to align with Ecuador's Organic Law of the National Public Procurement System (LOSNCP), particularly Articles 6, 25, and 31, which establish transparency, equity, and efficiency as guiding principles. Technical evaluation through simulations compares Ethereum and Algorand in terms of transaction costs, performance, and energy efficiency, justifying the a doption of Ethereum for its broader maturity. While results confirm the f easibility of the system, limitations remain, as validation relies on simulations rather than pilot deployments, and scalability under real-world conditions requires further study. By combining transparency, security, and regulatory compliance, this work contributes a scalable and innovative model for future e-government procurement systems that prioritize accountability, equity, and public trust.  Β© 2025 IEEE.
