AB  - Blockchain technology, implemented via smart contracts, provides significant improvements in data security, transparency, and automation within healthcare applications. Smart contracts are inherently unsuitable for critical healthcare settings. This is due to vulnerabilities such as reentrancy attacks, overflow concerns, and unauthorized access to sensitive data. This study introduces the Blockchain Enhanced Smart Contract Security Framework (BESCSF) to enhance the security of smart contracts in healthcare applications. Present research is making use of hybrid security architecture. Such architecture incorporates sophisticated cryptographic methods, secure coding standards, and real-time monitoring. The suggested architecture has an adaptive hybrid approach. This approach uses machine learning to predict potential vulnerabilities. Present work prioritizes resource allocation for auditing and safeguarding smart contracts. This paradigm is capable to enhance the efficacy and precision of security measures. Present work evaluates the framework's performance, contract execution accuracy along with ability to reduce security breaches. It is performed via a series of tests and simulations. The hybrid method significantly enhances vulnerability detection accuracy by 15%. Moreover present research reduces execution time by 20% compared to traditional security solutions. Healthcare applications are considered in preset research papers that are capable to prioritizing security, efficiency, and regulatory compliance. Research work considers medical supply chain monitoring, patient data management, and insurance claims. Present work would find the method ideal. Thus, it also enhances trust and scalability.  Β© 2025 IEEE.
