AB  - The rapid adoption of digital healthcare requires secure and efficient platforms for managing patient records. This study introduces a Centralized Patient Data System equipped with multi-layer security features to address issues such as fragmented data, weak verification methods, and delays in accessing critical medical information. This system integrates multiple verification layers, including RFID tags for doctors, patient biometrics such as fingerprint and facial scans, and an emergency OTP mechanism, to ensure only verified users can access records. Data integrity is maintained through the use of SHA-256 cryptographic hashing combined with a blockchain-style ledger, producing an auditable and tamper-evident data trail. In addition, the system integrates an AI-driven summarization module that condenses lengthy patient histories into concise reports, assisting physicians in making timely and informed decisions. Built on a NodeMCU microcontroller, the design connects biometric devices and RFID readers to a centralized server, enabling scalability across different healthcare environments. Experimental results indicate that the system effectively preserves confidentiality, resists data manipulation, and enhances clinical workflows, offering a reliable solution for modern digital healthcare management. Β© 2025 IEEE.
