AB  - Secure audit logging is essential for cloud forensics, yet existing schemes suffer from fundamental limitations. Classical hash-chaining, pairing-based, and RSA/ECC-based logging lose evidential validity under key compromise and quantum attacks, while blockchain-assisted approaches rely on pre-quantum primitives and periodic verification, leaving temporal windows exploitable by insiders. This paper presents Post-Quantum Attribute-Based Logging (PQ-ABL), a quantum-resilient forensic logging framework with continuous integrity assurance. PQ-ABL employs lattice-based CP-ABE to enforce fine-grained, post-quantum access control, while log payloads are efficiently protected using AES-GCM. Integrity is achieved through an epoch-driven verification model that anchors a single Merkle root per epoch on the blockchain, enabling linear-time O(N) full-epoch validation, constant-time O(1) local record consistency checking after epoch validation, and O(log N) standalone record membership verification, without requiring per-record authentication proofs during epoch validation. The key contribution is an integrity design that decouples post-quantum authorization from verification cost, eliminating forensic blind spots inherent in periodic anchoring. Security analysis proves confidentiality and integrity under standard lattice hardness assumptions. Experimental results show that PQ-ABL reduces verification latency by 31.8% and computation cost by 38.6% compared with existing post-quantum and blockchain-based logging schemes. Β© 2013 IEEE.
