AB  - Advances in telemedicine and the Internet of Medical Things (IoMT) have led to the decentralization of medical imaging systems, exposing sensitive patient data to increasing security risks, while also considering the hardware constraints of embedded devices and a strict regulatory framework. This work is a systematic comparative study of two emerging security paradigms: chaotic encryption enhanced by artificial intelligence and blockchain-based access control mechanisms, deployed in embedded healthcare environments. Four evaluation criteria were used: cryptographic robustness, computational load, scalability, and compliance with current standards. The results, obtained from real medical imaging datasets, reveal significant trade-offs. AI-assisted chaotic encryption hybrid approaches demonstrate remarkable real-time privacy efficiency, with informational entropy exceeding 7.997, correlation coefficients close to 0.001, and an average encryption time of 0.1 seconds for standardsized images. Simultaneously, blockchain ensures decentralized trust and irrefutable traceability, with a throughput of up to 109,248 transactions per second and an access latency of less than 500 ms. In-depth statistical analysis validates blockchain's superiority for auditing and regulatory compliance, thanks to its immutable ledgers, while chaotic methods guarantee robust pixel protection, with alteration rates exceeding 99.6%. Ultimately, this study identifies optimal hybrid architectures that reconcile these conflicting imperatives, paving the way for secure, efficient, and compliant distributed medical imaging systems. Β© 2025 IEEE.
