AB  - Fixed, Mobile, and Satellite Convergence (FMSC) networks aim to enable seamless communication across diverse infrastructure domains. Traditional access and roaming control mechanisms are centralized, inflexible, and unsuitable for heterogeneous environments. This research proposes a decentralized framework based on blockchain smart contracts to manage roaming agreements and dynamic access authorization across FMSC domains. The architecture introduces two core innovations: (1) a Roaming Agreement Smart Contract (RASC) for autonomous SLA negotiation between operators, and (2) an Access Authorization Contract (AAC) that issues time-bound tokens using decentralized identities (DIDs). By leveraging permissioned blockchains, the system achieves rapid token issuance while eliminating single points of failure inherent in conventional HSS/UDM architectures. The solution ensures secure, auditable interoperability through cryptographic identity binding and immutable policy enforcement, addressing critical gaps in multi-domain trust management. Key advancements include programmable QoS-aware access tokens, integration of intent-based FMSC orchestration systems, and a revocation mechanism for dynamic policy updates. The framework's modular design aligns with 3GPP and ITU-T standards while enabling new business models for cross-domain service delivery in 5G/6G and satellite networks.  Β© 2025 IEEE.
