AB  - Hashed Timelock Contracts (HTLCs) underpin atomic swaps and payment channel networks by combining a hashlock with a timelock, yet their economic soundness remains fragile under rational adversarial behavior. We analyze the griefing attack, in which an adversary withholds the preimage until expiry to immobilize an honest counterparty's liquidity at negligible cost, thereby imposing material opportunity loss without risking principal. To formalize this asymmetry, we develop a game-theoretic model together with a Python-based, discrete-time simulation framework that quantifies both the attacker's cost and the victim's lock duration under realistic protocol timelines. Building on this foundation, we propose the Commitment Bond HTLC (CB-HTLC), a lightweight mitigation that requires the initiator to post a small, non-refundable bond prior to lock creation. This bond introduces an unavoidable, upfront cost that converts strategic delay into a strictly loss-making action, restoring incentive alignment for rational participants. Empirical evaluation demonstrates that, whereas the classic HTLC permits zero-cost griefing with full-duration liquidity lock, CB-HTLC reshapes the payoff landscape: the attacker's inaction becomes economically irrational while atomicity and liveness for honest participants remain intact. Crucially, the mechanism is lightweight, protocol-agnostic, and immediately deployable, offering a rare example of a security enhancement that is both effective and practical without disruptive network-wide upgrades. Our contri-butions-formalization of griefing as a strategic game, development of an open simulation tool, and empirical evidence that commitment bonds realign incentives-advance HTLC-based systems toward genuine economic robustness rather than cryptographic sufficiency alone. Β© 2025 IEEE.
