AB  - A cryptocurrency wallet (crypto wallet), similar to a virtual bank account, allows users to store, send, and receive digital currency while also tracking their balance and transaction history. The user's private keys, which are required to access their digital assets, are protected. Crypto wallets are becoming more popular as they improve the user experience through improved interfaces and user-friendly designs. However, due to the increase in breaches and scams in the cryptocurrency market, security has risen to the top of wallet providers' priorities. As a result, several wallets now include enhanced security features such as two-factor authentication and biometric authentication. Low latency and lightweight crypto wallets are essential for quick and efficient bitcoin transactions. Low latency ensures quick confirmation of transactions, reducing the time required to complete a money transfer. Because they use fewer resources and are faster to download, install, and execute, lightweight wallets are more accessible and user-friendly, particularly for devices with limited storage and processing capacity. Blockchain technology is used in cutting-edge wallet solutions to provide unrivalled security while remaining computationally light. This positions blockchain as a crypto wallet facilitator, addressing the aforementioned challenges. The decentralised architecture of blockchain ensures the security and preservation of cryptographic assets. The distributed ledger speeds up processing, and light nodes keep the cryptocurrency wallet small. Β© 2023 IEEE.
